WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:15.262
Episode two: datasets. The pool from episode one is ready: a RAIDZ on three disks and a spare.

00:00:15.362 --> 00:00:35.928
A dataset is a file system cut from the pool. No size to decide, no mkfs, no fstab: it is mounted as soon as it exists, and nested datasets mount inside their parent.

00:00:36.028 --> 00:00:51.665
Properties are set on a dataset and inherited by its children. Turn on lz4 compression on projects: web and db inherit it. The SOURCE column says where each value comes from.

00:00:51.765 --> 00:01:07.183
Copy the system's documentation into web: text compresses well, and the compression ratio says it takes a third less space on the disks. lz4 is so cheap it is worth having everywhere.

00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:33.387
A quota is a ceiling: web may not grow past two hundred megabytes. Write two hundred and fifty: the write stops at the quota with Disk quota exceeded.

00:01:33.487 --> 00:01:56.439
Delete the file. ZFS frees the space in the background, a few seconds later: right after the delete, the dataset still looks full.

00:01:56.539 --> 00:02:12.667
A reservation is the opposite: space guaranteed to a dataset. Reserve five hundred megabytes for db: it holds nothing, yet the others can use five hundred megabytes less.

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:27.089
recordsize is the largest block of a file. A database writes small pages, so db gets sixteen kilobytes; files that are read whole keep the default, one hundred and twenty-eight.

00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:50.556
Set a property locally to override the parent, and zfs inherit to give it back: db without compression, then inheriting lz4 again.

00:02:50.656 --> 00:03:10.835
Where a dataset is mounted is a property too: web moves to /srv/web, with its files, in one command.

00:03:10.935 --> 00:03:17.833 line:6%
Next episode: snapshots and clones.
